  <action>
Wire ONE path end to end: `testdata/phase07/call_basic.lang`, a two-function
program where one function calls the other, admitted by `lang check` and handled
at every dispatch site. One call site, one callee, no other shapes.

**Parser.** In `internal/compiler/syntax/parser.go`'s `linearBody`, the branch
taken when `p.peek().Kind == TokenLParen` currently emits a
`syntax.fallible_call_not_consumed` problem and an
`ast.RHS{Kind: "call_unconsumed"}` binding unconditionally, before the callee's
identity is known. Replace the unconditional problem with an
`ast.RHS{Kind: "call", Callee: source.Text, Arguments: <from p.callArguments()>, Span: source.Span}`
binding. Callee identity is a check-time fact, so the parser now accepts the
shape and the admission decision relocates to `check`. Do not add a second
argument parser — `p.callArguments()` at `:459` already returns `([]string, int)`.

**AST.** In `internal/compiler/ast/ast.go`, extend the `Callee`/`Arguments`
doc comment to cover the new kind and state that `"call"` never sets `Rationale`.

**Core.** Add `OpCall OperationKind = "call"` to the kind block, with a doc
comment following `OpRelease`'s style: state that it is never a terminator, and
that it carries a single `TargetID` plus a callee identity, unlike
`OpForeignCall`'s two edges. Add `OpCall` to the `AllOperationKinds()` literal
slice. Do **not** add it to `TerminatorKinds()`.

**Check.** Add a `"call"` arm to the per-binding kind handling in
`analyzeStraightLine` and `analyzeArmBody`. Admission for this tracer: the callee
resolves to a declared Lang function name; exactly one argument; the argument
resolves in the `places` scope map (the function's own parameter or any prior
`let`) — not merely equal to the function parameter's name. Emit `core.OpCall`
with `SourceID` = the argument's place, `TargetID` = the binding's new place, and
the callee's function ID. Ownership convention is read implicitly from the
callee's declared `lang.interface/1` signature (D-07-02) — there is no call-site
syntax in which to express an override, so no diagnostic is needed to refuse one.
Populate the caller's `ClosureDigest` Merkle-chain arm over the callee's
`ClosureDigest` (the zero-callee base case landed in `07-01`).

**Corevalidate.** Add `case core.OpCall:` to **both** `replayStraightLine` and
`replayBlocks`, modeled on the `OpCopy` single-target shape, plus a callee-ID
resolution check reusing `isDeclaredFunctionName`'s precedence rule. Every new
case lands twice, per engine — do not extract a shared helper.

**Interp.** Add `case core.OpCall:` to `runBranchArm`, `runLinearBlocks`, and
`runLinear`, emitting through `ownedEvent`. No call-stack semantics this phase —
SEM-08 is Phase 10. Use `interp.go:97-107`'s documented-unreachable comment style
where the case cannot yet be exercised.

**Cgen.** Add `core.OpCall` to the four existing case lists (research reports
`:260, :467, :652, :1652`; CONTEXT.md reports `:259, :466, :651` — verify the live
line numbers before editing, trust neither citation blindly), returning the same
"not supported this phase" error shape as `cgen.go:1723-1729`. The doc comment
must cite the reachability argument: `Emit`/`EmitNative` hard-fail on
`len(Functions) != 1`, and no legal `OpCall`-bearing program has exactly one
function, so this arm is structurally unreachable this phase and is registered as
forward hygiene for Phase 11. Do not touch the `len(Functions) != 1` guard itself.
Recorded here so a Phase 11 reviewer does not have to rediscover it: **D-07-04** —
at arity 1 `cgen` can emit at most one `restrict` per callee, so the attribute is
provably correct but never load-bearing. Phase 11 must scope its `restrict`
evidence explicitly to single-parameter derivation and name the two-parameter case
as an M003 obligation, rather than implying two-pointer coverage it does not have.

**Pathoracle and originvalidate** have no exhaustive `OperationKind` switch —
"handled" for those two sites means their walk completes without error on an
`OpCall`-bearing function. Confirm that, do not add a switch.

**Fixture.** `testdata/phase07/call_basic.lang`: module `phase07.call_basic`, a
leading `//` comment naming D-07-01/D-07-02/SEM-04 and what the fixture proves,
an `export { fn ... }` block, and two functions where one calls the other with a
single in-scope binding as the argument. No golden `.c` sibling — `cgen` never
runs on a multi-function program.
