<threat_model>
## Trust Boundaries

| Boundary | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| source text -> parser | untrusted `.lang` input; the parser no longer blanket-refuses a bare call, so the shape it now admits must be re-refused downstream where identity is known |
| AST -> check | callee identity (foreign vs Lang vs unknown) is decided here for the first time |
| check -> corevalidate/interp/cgen | a `core.Program` carrying a new operation kind crosses into five consumers that each dispatch exhaustively |

## STRIDE Threat Register

| Threat ID | Category | Component | Severity | Disposition | Mitigation Plan |
|-----------|----------|-----------|----------|-------------|-----------------|
| T-07-07 | Elevation of privilege | relocated bare-call refusal | high | mitigate | Moving a refusal from parse time to check time risks losing it. Task 2 pins the outcome at all three existing assertion sites (`check_test.go:791`, `syntax_test.go:1005`, `native_test.go:1175`) with the diagnostic code preserved verbatim |
| T-07-08 | Tampering | `corevalidate` `default:` arms | high | mitigate | The fail-closed `default: core.unknown_operation` arms at `:1000-1002` and `:1214-1216` are not widened to absorb the new kind; an explicit `case core.OpCall:` is added at both sites so an unregistered kind still fails loudly |
| T-07-09 | Spoofing | call-site ownership convention | medium | mitigate | D-07-02: the convention is read implicitly from the callee's declared signature. There is no call-site syntax in which to express an override, so the standing anti-feature is ungrammatical rather than merely rejected — a caller structurally cannot weaken a callee's exclusivity claim |
| T-07-10 | Tampering | unresolvable callee name | high | mitigate | A callee resolving to neither a foreign symbol nor a declared Lang function is refused with the existing unknown-name code, never silently dropped. A dropped call is how a cycle later escapes detection |
| T-07-11 | Repudiation | exhaustive-dispatch controls | medium | mitigate | Both controls are literal phase-scoped lists (A-05). Phase 07 adds its own rather than repurposing Phase 4's, so a green Phase 4 lane cannot be mistaken for Phase 07 coverage. `cgen` non-coverage is stated explicitly |
| T-07-SC | Tampering | npm/pip/cargo/go installs | high | mitigate | No package-manager install task in this plan; 07-RESEARCH.md's Package Legitimacy Audit records zero new production dependencies and zero `[SLOP]`/`[SUS]` verdicts |
</threat_model>

