<threat_model>
## Trust Boundaries

| Boundary | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| check's in-memory core.Program -> the returned artifact | the real trust boundary is the *returned* artifact; a cyclic program exists only as an ephemeral local |
| forged/synthetic core.Program -> callgraph | `Order` must behave correctly on programs no parser produced |
| cycle witness -> published diagnostic ID | the ID is a versioned API consumed by `lang-repair` and `lang explain` |

## STRIDE Threat Register

| Threat ID | Category | Component | Severity | Disposition | Mitigation Plan |
|-----------|----------|-----------|----------|-------------|-----------------|
| T-07-16 | Denial of service | `callgraph.Order` on a dense or adversarial graph | high | mitigate | Edges deduped at graph-build time bounds E by V-squared; traversal is O(V+E) over an explicit stack, never native Go recursion, so a deep graph cannot blow the Go stack. The diagnostic is bounded at 32 causes; the traversal is not |
| T-07-17 | Denial of service | cyclic program reaching interp or cgen | high | mitigate | D-07-14: refusal runs over `check`'s own completed program and returns before the artifact escapes. A test asserts `check` returns no program on a cycle |
| T-07-18 | Tampering | forged artifact naming an unresolvable callee | high | mitigate | An `OpCall` whose callee resolves to nothing makes `Order` return an error, never drop the edge. A dropped edge is how a cycle escapes detection |
| T-07-19 | Tampering | diagnostic-ID instability enabling downstream dedup/repair confusion | high | mitigate | Canonical rotation of `cycle_member` causes before hashing (D-07-16), with a required test asserting one identical ID across two discovery-root orderings, plus a seeded mutation that removes the rotation |
| T-07-20 | Spoofing | node identity under foreign-symbol shadowing | high | mitigate | Node identity reuses `isDeclaredFunctionName`'s precedence rule (`corevalidate.go:1654-1664`) rather than restating it; a divergent rule is how an edge disappears. The fixture proving it lands in 07-05 |
| T-07-21 | Tampering | production code reachable through a fault-injection seam | medium | mitigate | Each seam is `nil` by default, read through a private accessor, doc-commented as test-only, and asserted restored by `defer` in every test that sets it. Stated explicitly because `callgraph`, unlike `pathoracle`, is a production `check` dependency |
| T-07-SC | Tampering | npm/pip/cargo/go installs | high | mitigate | No package-manager install task in this plan; the Package Legitimacy Audit records zero new production dependencies |
</threat_model>

